      "name": "ProcessFormatSpecifier",
      "signature": "int ProcessFormatSpecifier(uint param1, size_t bufferSize, char * formatSpec)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "undefined4",
      "comment": "Processes format specifiers for Unicode string transformations\nDispatches to different handlers based on format type (0x0e or 0x09)\nReturns 0 on success, 1 if format not recognized\n\nSummary:\nHandles format-based Unicode string processing with two main paths:\n- Format 0x0E: Validates source object, retrieves Unicode string, converts to win1252,\n  splits at specified offset, reformats with buffer text between parts, converts back to Unicode\n- Format 0x09: Manages conversion counter, increments and checks against limit, processes conversion\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load format specifier byte from param3\n2. If format == 0x0E:\n   a. Validate and retrieve Unicode object field\n   b. Initialize 256-element Unicode array constructor loop\n   c. Convert Unicode object to win1252 in unicodeText buffer\n   d. Calculate string lengths for both unicodeText and formatBuffer\n   e. Check combined length fits in 256 bytes\n   f. Copy first bufferSize chars from unicodeText to prefixBuffer\n   g. Copy remaining chars (after bufferSize offset) to suffixBuffer\n   h. Format: sprintf(unicodeText, \"%s%s%s\", prefixBuffer, formatBuffer, suffixBuffer)\n   i. Convert formatted string back to Unicode array\n   j. Call ProcessUnicodeWithCallback() to apply transformation\n   k. Return 0\n3. If format == 0x09:\n   a. Check if DAT_6fa0a08c counter limit exists\n   b. Increment DAT_6f9e9c48 counter\n   c. If counter >= limit, reset to 0\n   d. Call ConvertStringToUnicodeAndProcess() with counter value\n   e. Return 0\n4. Otherwise return 1 (invalid format)\n\nParameters:\n- param1 (uint): First parameter (context/state value)\n- bufferSize (size_t): Offset for string splitting in format 0x0E path\n- formatSpec (char *): Format specifier string starting with type byte\n\nReturns:\n- 0: Format processed successfully\n- 1: Format specifier not recognized (not 0x0E or 0x09)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Format 0x0E requires valid object field and combined string length <= 255 bytes\n- If validation fails, function exits early without processing\n- Counter in format 0x09 wraps to 0 when exceeding limit\n- All string operations are null-terminated",
