      "name": "HandleEmoteChatCommand",
      "signature": "DWORD HandleEmoteChatCommand(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "undefined4",
      "comment": "Handles the \"/me\" emote chat command, formatting and broadcasting player emote messages.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Retrieve the chat message string via ValidateAndGetObjectField()\n2. Validate pointer is non-null, return 1 if invalid\n3. Get string length via strlen() and check for non-empty\n4. Convert Unicode string to Windows ANSI format into 300-byte buffer\n5. Check if first character is '/' (slash indicates command, not emote)\n6. Get system tick count via GetTickCount()\n7. Load last throttle timestamp from DAT_6fa09f10\n8. Calculate next allowed time: lastTime + 1000ms throttle interval\n9. Compare current tick against next allowed time (JC skips if too soon)\n10. Update DAT_6fa09f10 with current tick count\n11. Format message as \"/me %s\" using sprintf with converted string\n12. Call FUN_6f9dd5b0() to broadcast the formatted emote message\n13. Return 1 (success) to caller\n\nParameters:\nNone - Function uses implicit ECX context from caller\n\nReturns:\nDWORD - Always returns 1 (success indicator)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Null/invalid string pointer: skips processing, returns 1\n- Empty string: skips processing, returns 1\n- Message starts with '/': treated as command, not emote, skips processing\n- Throttling: limits emote broadcasts to 1 per 1000ms (1 second)\n- Stack usage: 600 bytes for two 300-byte Unicode conversion buffers\n- Global state: DAT_6fa09f10 tracks last broadcast time for throttle check\n\nMessage Format:\n- Input: arbitrary player-typed text (Unicode)\n- Output: \"/me <text>\" format sent to broadcast handler\n- Filtering: slash-prefixed text bypasses emote formatting",
