      "name": "CleanupUIControlsForState",
      "signature": "void CleanupUIControlsForState(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "comment": "Cleans up and destroys UI control structures based on current game mode state.\\n\\nAlgorithm:\\n1. Load game mode state flag from DAT_6fa09dac\\n2. Test if state flag is non-zero (cleanup needed)\\n3. If state == 1: Destroy mode 1 UI controls (0x6fa09d64, 0x6fa09d68, 0x6fa09d6c, 0x6fa09d70, 0x6fa09d74)\\n4. If state != 1 (but nonzero): Destroy mode 2+ UI controls (0x6fa09d78, 0x6fa09d7c, 0x6fa09d80, 0x6fa09d84, 0x6fa09d88, 0x6fa09d8c)\\n5. Clear state flag (DAT_6fa09dac = 0)\\n6. Destroy common UI controls shared by all modes (0x6fa09d90, 0x6fa09d94, 0x6fa09d98, 0x6fa09d9c, 0x6fa09da0, 0x6fa09da4, 0x6fa09da8)\\n7. Restore previous global game state from DAT_6fa09160\\n8. Check if DAT_6fa0a0d0 contains a valid UI control pointer\\n9. If control exists, set it as the active UI control\\n\\nReturns:\\nvoid: No return value\\n\\nSpecial Cases:\\n- State flag 0 (cleanup not needed): Skips all destroy calls, jumps directly to state restoration\\n- State flag 1 vs other nonzero: Two different cleanup paths for different UI configurations\\n- DAT_6fa0a0d0 may be null: Only sets active control if pointer is non-zero\\n- Magic number 0x1: State comparison threshold for control set selection",
